/** @page midistreamplayer_overview MidiStreamPlayer Overview
 *  @brief Choose MidiStreamPlayer when MIDI messages are created live at runtime.
 *
 *  @details
 *  Use MidiStreamPlayer when your application generates MIDI messages dynamically.
 *  It is the best fit when notes depend on user input, game state, procedural rules,
 *  or simulation data available only at runtime.
 *
 *  Compared with MidiFilePlayer:
 *  - MidiFilePlayer is optimized for complete MIDI song playback.
 *  - MidiStreamPlayer is optimized for event-by-event live control.
 *
 *  @section msp_overview_quick_start Quick Start
 *  Place the MidiStreamPlayer prefab in your scene, get a reference in script,
 *  then send messages with @ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayEvent "MPTK_PlayEvent()".
 *
 *  @section msp_overview_decision_guide Decision Guide
 *  Prefer MidiStreamPlayer when:
 *  - timing is driven by gameplay code,
 *  - MIDI messages are generated one by one or in small batches,
 *  - you need immediate control over channels/controllers/notes.
 *
 *  Prefer MidiFilePlayer when:
 *  - you want full-song sequencing from existing MIDI files,
 *  - transport controls (seek, loop, timeline) are central,
 *  - you consume events from loaded MIDI content.
 *
 *  @see @ref midistreamplayer_realtime
 *  @see @ref mptkevent_concept
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayEvent
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_StopEvent
 */

/** @page midistreamplayer_realtime Real-Time MIDI Messages Workflow
 *  @brief Create, send, and stop MPTKEvent instances at runtime.
 *
 *  @details
 *  The core flow is:
 *  - create an @ref MidiPlayerTK.MPTKEvent "MPTKEvent",
 *  - set command and required message data (channel/value/velocity/duration),
 *  - call @ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayEvent "MPTK_PlayEvent()",
 *  - stop sustained notes with @ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_StopEvent "MPTK_StopEvent()" when needed.
 *
 *  @section msp_rt_single Single-Event Flow
 *  This is ideal for keyboard keys, game triggers, and one-shot responses.
 *
 *  @section msp_rt_batch Batch Flow
 *  For grouped actions, pass a `List<MPTKEvent>` to
 *  @ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayEvent "MPTK_PlayEvent()".
 *  This is useful for short phrases, layered notes, and synchronized bursts.
 *
 *  @section msp_rt_common_mistakes Common Mistakes
 *  - Missing channel/velocity/value for NoteOn events.
 *  - Using very long durations without explicit stop strategy.
 *  - Mixing file-playback expectations (transport timeline) with live event workflows.
 *
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayEvent
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_StopEvent
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MPTKEvent
 *  @see @ref mptkevent_concept
 */

/** @page midistreamplayer_chords Chords, Scales, and Live Harmony
 *  @brief Build harmonic content live from scales or chord-library definitions.
 *
 *  @details
 *  In Maestro Pro, MidiStreamPlayer can build chords from:
 *  - the current scale (@ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromScale "MPTK_PlayChordFromScale"),
 *  - the chord library (@ref MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromLib "MPTK_PlayChordFromLib").
 *
 *  @section msp_chord_builder Chord Configuration Model
 *  Chords are configured with @ref MidiPlayerTK.MPTKChordBuilder "MPTKChordBuilder", including tonic, channel,
 *  velocity, duration, arpeggio spacing, and delay.
 *
 *  @section msp_chord_usage Typical Usage
 *  - gameplay-driven harmony layers,
 *  - procedural accompaniment,
 *  - adaptive tension/release patterns based on game state.
 *
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromScale
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromLib
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_StopChord
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MPTKChordBuilder
 */

/** @page midistreamplayer_pro MidiStreamPlayer Pro Extensions
 *  @brief Extended expressive controls for advanced live playback workflows.
 *
 *  @details
 *  Maestro Pro adds stream-focused capabilities such as:
 *  - pitch wheel control with normalized values,
 *  - configurable pitch bend sensitivity,
 *  - runtime scale selection and scale-based chord building,
 *  - chord-library playback helpers.
 *
 *  @section msp_pro_use_cases Typical Pro Use Cases
 *  - expressive instrument control (bend + sensitivity),
 *  - adaptive harmony systems,
 *  - procedural generation pipelines requiring scale-aware output.
 *
 *  These features are designed for expressive performance controls,
 *  adaptive game music, and advanced procedural composition workflows.
 *
 *  @see @ref maestro_pro_features
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayPitchWheelChange
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayPitchWheelSensitivity
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_ScaleSelected
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromScale
 *  @see MidiPlayerTK.MidiStreamPlayer.MPTK_PlayChordFromLib
 */
